Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a quick “road‑map” of the elements that matter for this task, followed by ten exploratory / incremental Snowflake SQL queries (each capped at 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the outline of a final analytic query.  
All identifiers are wrapped in double quotation marks as required.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Relevant columns and typical usage / conditions

1. WORLD_PORT_INDEX  (NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS)
   • "region_number" – filter to ‘6585’.  
   • "country"        – keep only 'US'.  
   • "port_name"      – output field.  
   • "port_geom"      – WKB geometry used for spatial joins.  
   • "port_latitude", "port_longitude" – fallback for generating a GEOGRAPHY point.

2. STATES  (NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES)
   • "state_geom"     – polygon boundary for each state (GEOGRAPHY/WKB).  
   • "state_name"     – output.  
   • "state"          – 2‑letter code, useful for joins / display.

3. HURRICANES  (NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES)
   • "basin"          – filter to ‘NA’ (North Atlantic).  
   • "usa_wind"       – ≥ 35 kts.  
   • "usa_sshs"       – ≥ 0 (≥ minimal tropical‑storm strength).  
   • "name"           – storm name (output, DISTINCT list).  
   • "season"         – storm year (output).  
   • "sid"            – unique storm identifier (for counting).  
   • "latitude", "longitude" – storm centre; build a GEOGRAPHY point.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
10 sample Snowflake SQL queries  
(Each block starts with a short comment, then the query.)

```sql
-- 1) Peek at all ports in region 6585
SELECT DISTINCT
       "port_name",
       "region_number",
       "country"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX
WHERE  "region_number" = '6585'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Same ports but only those explicitly marked as U.S.
SELECT
       "port_name",
       "country",
       "region_number"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX
WHERE  "region_number" = '6585'
  AND  "country" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Show geometry (as WKT) for a few target ports
SELECT
       "port_name",
       ST_ASWKT( TO_GEOGRAPHY("port_geom") )     AS "port_wkt"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX
WHERE  "region_number" = '6585'
  AND  "country" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Inspect state polygons & names (sanity check)
SELECT
       "state",
       "state_name",
       ST_AREA( TO_GEOGRAPHY("state_geom") )     AS "state_area_m2"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Sample spatial test: which ports fall inside which states?
SELECT
       p."port_name",
       s."state_name"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX  p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES                  s
  ON   ST_CONTAINS( TO_GEOGRAPHY(s."state_geom"),
                    TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom") )
WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
  AND  p."country" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Extract North‑Atlantic storms meeting wind & category criteria
SELECT DISTINCT
       "sid",
       "name",
       "season",
       "usa_wind",
       "usa_sshs"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE  "basin"      = 'NA'
  AND  "usa_wind"  >= 35         -- ≥ 35 kts
  AND  "usa_sshs"  >= 0          -- ≥ minimal tropical storm
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Turn each qualifying storm point into a GEOGRAPHY object
SELECT
       "sid",
       "name",
       "season",
       GEOGRAPHY_POINT("longitude", "latitude")  AS "storm_geom"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE  "basin"      = 'NA'
  AND  "usa_wind"  >= 35
  AND  "usa_sshs"  >= 0
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Very coarse proximity test: storm centres within 100 km of a port
--    (ST_DISTANCE returns metres for GEOGRAPHY)
SELECT
       p."port_name",
       s."name"      AS "storm_name",
       s."season",
       ST_DISTANCE( TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"),
                    GEOGRAPHY_POINT(s."longitude", s."latitude") ) AS "dist_m"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX  p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES               s
  ON   ST_DISTANCE( TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"),
                    GEOGRAPHY_POINT(s."longitude", s."latitude") ) <= 100000   -- 100 km
WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
  AND  p."country" = 'US'
  AND  s."basin"  = 'NA'
  AND  s."usa_wind" >= 35
  AND  s."usa_sshs" >= 0
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Aggregate storm counts & details per port (early draft)
SELECT
       p."port_name",
       ARRAY_AGG( DISTINCT s."season" )              AS "years_impacted",
       COUNT( DISTINCT s."sid" )                     AS "distinct_storms",
       ARRAY_AGG( DISTINCT s."name" )                AS "storm_names",
       AVG( s."usa_sshs" )                          AS "avg_category",
       AVG( s."usa_wind" )                          AS "avg_wind_kts"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX  p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES               s
  ON   ST_DISTANCE( TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"),
                    GEOGRAPHY_POINT(s."longitude", s."latitude") ) <= 100000
WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
  AND  p."country" = 'US'
  AND  s."basin"  = 'NA'
  AND  s."usa_wind" >= 35
  AND  s."usa_sshs" >= 0
GROUP BY
       p."port_name"
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Final‑form query sketch: ports in region 6585, lying inside U.S. state
--     polygons, summarising qualifying North‑Atlantic storms, returning
--     state name plus geometries for both port & storm points
SELECT
       p."port_name",
       st."state_name",
       ARRAY_AGG( DISTINCT s."season" )                        AS "years_impacted",
       COUNT( DISTINCT s."sid" )                               AS "distinct_storms",
       ARRAY_AGG( DISTINCT s."name" )                          AS "storm_names",
       AVG( s."usa_sshs" )                                    AS "avg_category",
       AVG( s."usa_wind" )                                    AS "avg_wind_kts",
       ST_ASWKT( TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom") )                 AS "port_geom_wkt",
       ARRAY_AGG( DISTINCT ST_ASWKT(
                   GEOGRAPHY_POINT(s."longitude", s."latitude") ) )
           AS "storm_geoms_wkt"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX  p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES                 st
  ON   ST_CONTAINS( TO_GEOGRAPHY(st."state_geom"),
                    TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom") )
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES               s
  ON   ST_DISTANCE( TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"),
                    GEOGRAPHY_POINT(s."longitude", s."latitude") ) <= 100000    -- adjust radius
WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
  AND  p."country" = 'US'
  AND  s."basin"  = 'NA'
  AND  s."usa_wind" >= 35
  AND  s."usa_sshs" >= 0
GROUP BY
       p."port_name",
       st."state_name"
LIMIT  20;
```

These queries progress from basic inspection (1‑4), through spatial joins and storm filtering (5‑8), to aggregation logic (9) and a complete answer blueprint (10). Adjust buffer radius and field names as needed after validating intermediate results.
